==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|révolter}}, from {{der|en|it|rivoltare}}, itself either from {{m|it|ri-}} with the verb {{m|it|voltare}}, or possibly from a {{der|en|VL.||*revoltāre}} < {{m|la||*revolvitāre}}, for {{m|la||*revolūtāre}}, frequentative of {{der|en|la|revolvō|t=roll back}} (through its past participle {{m|la|revolūtus}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈvoʊlt/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈvəʊlt/}}
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈvɒlt/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-revolt.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊlt}}
* {{hyph|en|re|volt}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[rebel]], particularly against authority.
#: {{ux|en|The farmers had to '''revolt''' against the government to get what they deserved.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King John|act=V|scene=i|passage=Our discontented counties do '''revolt'''.}}
# To [[repel]] greatly.
#: {{ux|en|Your brother '''revolts''' me!}}
#* {{rfdatek|en|Burke}}
#*: This abominable medley is made rather to '''revolt''' young and ingenuous minds.
#* {{rfdatek|en|J. Morley}}
#*: To derive delight from what inflicts pain on any sentient creature '''revolted''' his conscience and offended his reason.
# To cause to turn back; to roll or drive back; to put to flight.
#: {{rfquotek|en|Spenser}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be disgusted, shocked, or grossly offended; hence, to feel nausea; used with ''at''.
#: {{ux|en|The stomach '''revolts''' at such food; his nature '''revolts''' at cruelty.}}
# To turn away; to abandon or reject something; specifically, to turn away, or shrink, with abhorrence.
#* {{rfdatek|en|John Milton}}
#*: Still '''revolt''' when truth would set them free.
#* {{rfdatek|en|J. Morley}}
#*: His clear intelligence '''revolted''' from the dominant sophisms of that time.

